    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>The split rail fence is a classic of the American landscape, valued for its rustic look, relatively simple build and the way it marks a boundary without blocking the view. In the UK the closest equivalent is the post and rail fence: morticed posts with cleft or sawn rails slotted through them, common around paddocks and rural boundaries. Whatever you call it, what looks like a simple "stack and post" project has more technical detail than it seems, and ignoring it can lead to early rot, rails that fall out or a dispute over the boundary.</p>
<p>This guide covers the mistakes that matter most for split rail and post and rail fences specifically, and how to avoid them, so the fence stays straight and sound for as long as possible.</p>

<h2 id="wood-quality">1. Choosing the wrong timber</h2>
<p>A common mistake is choosing on price alone rather than on species and treatment. Split rail fences are usually made from a naturally durable timber or from pressure-treated softwood.</p>
<p>Cheaper softwood that has not been properly treated or dried is prone to warping, twisting and shrinking. As the rails dry and move, they can work loose from the post mortices. Naturally durable timbers contain extractives that help them resist rot and insects: in the UK, oak and sweet chestnut are the traditional choices for post and rail fencing, while in the US cedar and locust are common.</p>
<p><strong>How to avoid it:</strong> buy from a supplier who can tell you the species and treatment. If you choose treated timber, make sure the posts are treated for ground contact, not just above-ground use: in the UK look for Use Class 4, and in the US look for a ground-contact rating such as UC4A or UC4B. Ask the supplier which treatment and grade suit your site rather than assuming one grade is right everywhere. A better timber costs more upfront, but it usually lasts noticeably longer than a cheap one.</p>

<!--figure:weathered-split-rail-post|Untreated or poor timber weathers and rots quickly where it meets the ground.-->

<h2 id="post-depth">2. Setting posts too shallow</h2>
<p>A split rail fence relies entirely on its posts. There is no continuous top rail or cladding tying the line together, so each post has to stand on its own against wind, frost heave and soil movement.</p>
<p>Digging shallow holes to save time is a frequent mistake. In cold climates, moisture in the soil freezes and expands, pushing shallow posts upwards. Over a few winters the fence line can end up looking jagged rather than straight.</p>
<p><strong>How to avoid it:</strong> set posts commonly around a third of the post length in the ground, deeper in exposed or frost-prone ground, and follow your supplier's guidance for your post size, soil and climate. Where the ground freezes hard, posts should go below the local frost depth. A post-hole auger helps keep holes consistent. Full concrete is not always needed: many fencers prefer a gravel base and tightly tamped gravel and soil backfill, which drains better than concrete that can hold water against the timber. For the general method of setting posts, see our guide on <a href="/blog/how-to-build-a-wood-fence">how to build a wood fence</a>.</p>

<h2 id="drainage">3. Ignoring drainage</h2>
<p>Water is the biggest enemy of any timber fence. Running the fence through a low, wet part of the garden or field without dealing with drainage is a common error. If the base of the posts sits in standing water after rain, even well-treated timber will eventually rot at ground level.</p>
<p>Another mistake is finishing the backfill in a dip around the post, which makes a little cup that channels rain straight down the side of the post.</p>
<p><strong>How to avoid it:</strong> watch how water moves across your land before you dig. If the fence has to cross a wet hollow, adjust the post positions or the rail heights so timber is kept out of the wettest ground where you can. When backfilling, finish with a slight mound of soil or concrete that slopes away from the post so water runs off rather than collecting at its base.</p>

<h2 id="rail-fit">4. Digging every hole before fitting the rails</h2>
<p>The "split" in split rail refers to the rough, hand-split character of the timber, and cleft rails in particular are not all exactly the same length. A common mistake is digging every post hole first to a theoretical spacing and then trying to fit the rails.</p>
<p>Because rails vary, some will barely reach into the mortice while others overlap too far. Rails without enough bite in the post can be knocked out by strong wind, livestock or a large dog leaning on them.</p>
<p><strong>How to avoid it:</strong> use the "dig-as-you-go" method. Set the first post, slot in its rails, then use those actual rails to mark the position of the next hole. Make sure there is enough rail inside each mortice that it can't be pushed out, typically several centimetres. This keeps the whole line sound and saves re-digging holes that are just slightly out.</p>

<!--figure:rustic-zigzag-rail-fence|Rails need to fit the post mortises snugly, so set posts as you go rather than all at once.-->

<h2 id="boundary-lines">5. Guessing the boundary</h2>
<p>Few mistakes are as costly as a fence in the wrong place. Old plans, hedges and "everyone knows" landmarks often do not match the legal boundary, and a fence that strays onto a neighbour's land may have to come down.</p>
<p><strong>How to avoid it:</strong> establish the boundary before you buy materials. In England and Wales, start with your title plan from HM Land Registry (bearing in mind it usually shows general boundaries only), check your deeds and talk to your neighbour; in the US, a licensed surveyor can mark your property corners. Then set the fence a little inside your boundary so a leaning post still stays on your land and you can maintain the far side. Our <a href="/blog/privacy-fence-mistakes">privacy fence mistakes</a> guide covers boundaries, planning rules and buried services in more detail.</p>

<h2 id="gate-reinforcement">6. Hanging a gate on a line post</h2>
<p>Gates are the only moving part of a fence and usually the first to fail. Hanging a gate on a standard line post is a common mistake: the weight and leverage of the gate gradually pull the post out of line, so the gate sags and drags on the ground.</p>
<p>Leaving out a diagonal brace is the other problem. Without one, the gate frame slowly racks out of square.</p>
<p><strong>How to avoid it:</strong> use a heavier gate post on the hinge side, set deeper and more firmly than the line posts (this is one place where a concrete footing often makes sense), and a matching post on the latch side. Brace the gate with a diagonal running from the bottom hinge corner to the top latch corner, which keeps the frame square and stops it sagging. Use galvanised or powder-coated hinges and latches sized for the gate.</p>

<h2 id="protective-finishes">7. Skipping a protective finish</h2>
<p>Because split rail fences are prized for their natural look, many people assume they need no maintenance. Durable and treated timbers can be left unfinished, but they weather to a silvery grey and can develop cracks, known as checking. Fine checking is mostly cosmetic, but deep cracks hold water and can encourage rot inside the timber.</p>
<p><strong>How to avoid it:</strong> decide whether you want the weathered look or a finish. If you want to finish the fence, follow the timber supplier's advice on how long to let the wood dry first, then apply an exterior oil or stain with UV protection, following the product label for preparation, application and how often to recoat. A clear finish helps keep the natural colour, while a semi-transparent stain adds a little tone. Check the fence each year and refresh the finish when it starts to wear.</p>

<!--figure:grey-weathered-rail-fence|Without a protective finish, rails turn grey and start to check and split.-->

<h2 id="wire-backing">8. Forgetting wire mesh until afterwards</h2>
<p>Split rail fences look good but on their own will not keep in small pets or keep out rabbits. A common mistake is realising this only after the fence is built, when adding mesh can be awkward and look untidy.</p>
<p><strong>How to avoid it:</strong> if you have dogs or a garden to protect, plan for mesh backing from the start. Black-coated welded mesh is far less visible than bright galvanised wire and keeps the rustic look. Fix it to the inside (your side) of the posts and rails with galvanised staples. For rabbits, the mesh needs to be buried or turned outwards at the base so they cannot dig under it. If the fence is for horses or livestock, follow specialist equine or agricultural fencing guidance, which sets its own rules for mesh, heights and fixings; barbed wire, for example, should not be used near horses.</p>

<blockquote><p><strong>Tip:</strong> Lay out all the rails along the fence line before you dig, sorted by length. Pairing similar rails in each bay makes the dig-as-you-go method quicker and keeps the fence line looking even.</p></blockquote>

<h2 id="faq">FAQ</h2>
<h3>How long does a split rail fence last?</h3>
<p>It depends on the timber, the treatment, your soil and climate, the quality of installation and how well it is maintained. Naturally durable timbers and properly ground-contact-treated posts generally outlast untreated or above-ground-rated softwood. Check the supplier's or manufacturer's guidance and any warranty for its stated term.</p>
<h3>Do I need concrete for split rail fence posts?</h3>
<p>Not always. Many fencers set line posts in tamped gravel and soil, which drains well. Concrete is more often used for gate posts or in loose, sandy soil where extra stability is needed. Follow your supplier's guidance for your ground.</p>
<h3>Can I install a split rail fence on a slope?</h3>
<p>Yes. Split rail fences follow the ground well: the rails can sit at an angle in the mortices so the fence runs with the contour of the slope rather than stepping down it.</p>
<h3>Why are my fence rails turning grey?</h3>
<p>Sunlight and weather break down the surface of the wood, which turns it a silvery grey. It is natural and many people like the look. If you want to keep the original colour, apply an exterior finish with UV protection, following the product label.</p>
<h3>What affects the cost of a split rail fence?</h3>
<p>The main factors are the timber species and treatment, the number of rails per bay (two-rail or three-rail), the length of the fence, the number of gates, any mesh backing and how difficult the ground is to dig. For comparable quotes, ask each installer to itemise the same specification, including posts, rails, gates, mesh and removal of any old fence.</p>

<h2 id="final-thoughts">Final thoughts</h2>
<p>Most split rail fence problems come down to preparation: the right timber, deep enough posts, good drainage, rails that fit their mortices, a confirmed boundary and a properly braced gate. Get those right and the fence will stay straight and keep its character for years. For more on timber fencing, see our <a href="/blog/wood-fence-pros-and-cons">wood fence pros and cons</a> and <a href="/blog/privacy-fence-mistakes">privacy fence mistakes</a> guides, or explore our <a href="/services/balcony-terrace-decoration">balcony and terrace decoration</a> service. To plan your garden or outdoor space with a designer, <a href="/book-consultation">book a consultation</a>.</p>
